GLS@MIT-AI 07/11/80 21:14:25 Re: SCHEME Chip 3 GC
To: GJS at MIT-AI, TK at MIT-AI, HES at MIT-AI, GLS at MIT-AI
To: H at MIT-AI, KRD at MIT-AI, SCHIP3 at MIT-AI
Note that with a copying GC the extra GC bit can go away.  Feature!

So I propose the following protocol.  There is an SBUS operation called
RELOCATE.  It operates like CAR: a pointer is fed across the bus, and a
result obtained.  The argument should be an "oldspace pointer".  Whatever
processor is responsible for that pointer should return the "new" pointer
for that object.  This may or may not cause copying, depending on the
device.  Another SBUS operation is a broadcast message, called FLIP.
This should cause all interested processors to exchange oldspace and newspace.
Also, there is an SBUS line that anyone can assert (wire-OR), called
GC-IN-PROGRESS.

Here is a full GC protocol.  Suppose that on the SBUS are two devices.
One is a UNIFY device with half-a-dozen registers, each a fake list cell
with a CAR and CDR, at addresses 40000000 through 40000006.  The other is
an APL device that has its own funny arrays, to which other people have
access through headers that look like LISP lists and arrays; these funny
arrays may contain numbers, characters, or pointers!  Objects in the
APL device appear to be at addresses 41000000-41777777.

So.  Either the APL machine or the on-chip storage allocator can signal
for a GC interrupt via the interrupt mechanism.  Eventually EVAL takes
the interrupt and runs a program that does whatever it wants, including
asking all other processors to get into a good state to GC, and then
executes the GC instruction.  This instruction creates a control-point
(thereby summarizing EVAL's state by an existing mechanism), broadcasts
the FLIP message across the SBUS, and hands the control state to the
storage allocator with the operation request RELOCATE.  This will return
fairly quickly (as soon as the storage allocator has copied the first
cell of the control state), but then EVAL loops until the GC-IN-PROGRESS
line on the SBUS is no longer asserted.

Now the APL processor has its own copying garbage collector, and so does
the on-chip allocator.  As long as the copy-queue-pointer lags behind the
freespace pointer of a gc'ing processor, it will assert GC-IN-PROGRESS;
when they are equal (that processor is caught up) it no longer asserts
it.  When a processor is asserting GC-IN-PROGRESS it should be performing
the standard copy loop.  When it runs across a pointer that points within
itself, it should leave the SBUS alone and do the copying internally.
When it finds a pointer that points outside its own range of addresses,
it should send it on the SBUS with the operation RELOCATE.  Whatever
processor is responsible for that pointer will respond with the new
address.  This pointer is then installed back where it was found.

Whether a processor is copying or just waiting for everyone else to
finish, it should pay attention to the SBUS for RELOCATE messages
directed to it.  The APL processor might get a message from the LISP
processor, for example, in which case it must copy the array pointed to
and return the new address.  Simple devices such as UNIFY respond to a
RELOCATE message by returning the argument as is, and then issuing two
RELOCATE messages (if necessary) for the car and cdr of the register just
asked about.  (This simple protocol, no worse than responding to CAR,
makes it so that EVAL doesn't have to explicitly save the state of all
the simple processors.)

Note that a processor might be done, and then have to reassert GC-IN-PROGRESS
because of a new RELOCATE message directed to it.  Eventually, however,
no processor will assert that signal, whereupon EVAL will start up
again, and feed NIL to the relocated control point, whereupon the GC
instruction returns NIL and everything carries on.  Hurray!

What do you think?

